CHMOD(1)                       P32W Unix                        CHMOD(1)

NAME
       chmod - change the mode of files

SYNOPSIS
       chmod [-R] mode file ...

DESCRIPTION
       chmod sets the mode of each file. The mode is an octal number
       built by adding:

       4000   set user id on execution
       2000   set group id on execution
       1000   sticky: in a directory, only a file's owner (or root)
              may remove or rename it
       0400   read by owner
       0200   write by owner
       0100   execute (search, for a directory) by owner
       0070   read, write, execute by group, the same way
       0007   read, write, execute by others

       or a symbolic mode: clauses separated by commas, each
       [who][op][perms], where who is any of u (owner), g (group),
       o (others) and a (all); op is + (add), - (take away) or =
       (set exactly); and perms is any of r, w, x, s (set-id), t
       (sticky) and X (execute, but only for a directory or a file
       that some x bit is set on already), or one of u, g, o to
       copy that class's rwx bits. Several ops may follow one who
       (u+r-w). With no who the bits the umask has set are left
       alone (chmod +x f gives rwxr-xr-x under umask 022).

       Only the owner of a file, or root, may change its mode.

OPTIONS
       -R     change directories recursively, and everything in
              them (a symbolic mode is worked out for each file).

EXAMPLES
       chmod 755 prog                rwxr-xr-x
       chmod 644 notes.txt           rw-r--r--
       chmod u+x,go-w script         executable, not writable by others
       chmod -R go= /usr/ken         a private home

EXIT STATUS
       0 if every mode was changed; 1 if any could not be (chmod
       says why and goes on); 2 for a usage error or a mode that is
       neither ("chmod: invalid mode: m").

SEE ALSO
       ls(1), chown(1), chmod(2), stat(2), umask(2)

BUGS
       The only option is -R, and it must come first; a mode that
       starts with - (such as -w) is taken as a mode.
